– Z-INDEX

Definition:

z-index is a CSS property that decides which element appears in front when two or more elements overlap.

Syntax:

selector {
position: value;
z-index: number;
}

Example:

.box1 {
position: absolute;
z-index: 1;
}

.box2 {
position: absolute;
z-index: 2;
}

Here, Box 2 appears in front of Box 1 because its z-index is higher.

Important:

Higher z-index = appears in front

Lower z-index = appears behind

Real-world example:

Imagine two cards placed on top of each other.
The card with a higher z-index will appear above the other card.

Common values:

z-index: 1;
z-index: 2;
z-index: 10;
z-index: 100;

Memory:

z-index = front / back
Higher number = front
Lower number = back
